Hi Taylor / Jordan — attaching the SDK quickstart and a short node/go snippet below to unblock your engineers. I also added an endpoint migration checklist PDF (high level) and a sample traffic shift timeline we usually follow for low-latency services.

Node (curl-like example using our sdk):
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_API_KEY, region: 'us-west-2' });
(async () => {
  const res = await client.generate({ model: 'rw-small:2025-06', prompt: 'Summarize...'});
  console.log(res.text);
})();

Go (minimal):
package main
import (
  "fmt"
  rw "github.com/redwoodai/sdk"
)
func main() {
  client := rw.NewClient(rw.WithAPIKeyEnv())
  out, _ := client.Generate(rw.GenerateRequest{Model: "rw-small:2025-06", Prompt: "Summarize..."})
  fmt.Println(out.Text)
}

Endpoint migration checklist (highlights):
- Provision sandbox endpoints and match headers/auth to prod
- Mirror traffic in staging and capture latency/seq-length distributions
- Run prefix/KV cache warmup with synthetic replay before cutover
- Configure routing fallback to previous provider for first 72 hours
- Validate observability (token-level cost, per-route latency) before / after

Attachments: rw-sdk-quickstart-samples.zip (zip, 1.2MB), endpoint-migration-checklist.pdf (pdf, 85KB)

If helpful I can run a 40-minute dev session during the workshop to walk through the snippets.

— Monica Patel
Customer Success Manager, Redwood Inference
